{% comment %}
  sections/home-spend-save.liquid
  Ported from the "Arcane Flame Website Prototype" option-c.html:
    - CSS: lines ~325-339 (the "spend ladder" comment block): .ladder,
      .rung, .rung:last-child, .rung:hover, .rung__pct, .rung__spend,
      .rung__bar, .rung__bar i, .reveal.in .rung__bar i, .shipflag, .terms
      — plus the .ladder-relevant slice of the <=1080px responsive rule
      (line ~387-389: `.ladder{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}` from
      the combined `.svc,.pgrid,.rev,.ladder,.foot__grid` selector, and
      the `.rung:nth-child(2n){border-right:0}` /
      `.rung:nth-child(-n+2){border-bottom:...}` rules that only apply to
      `.rung`) and the <=680px slice (line ~413:
      `.rung{border-right:0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(18,18,18,.14)}`
      from the combined `.ladder .rung, ...` rule at that breakpoint —
      see shopify-theme/assets/home-spend-save.css for the full colour-
      substitution notes.
    - Markup: lines ~703-726 (<section class="sec sec--light">...
      </section>) — the "Discounts that stack as your army grows"
      eyebrow/heading, the 4-rung discount ladder, the free-shipping
      flag banner and the minimum-spend disclaimer.

  ** CONTENT-ACCURACY FLAG (does not block this task, but needs a human
  check before publish): the 5%/10%/15%/20% discount percentages, the
  £10/£20/£30/£40 spend thresholds and the £50 free-shipping threshold
  below are the prototype's illustrative marketing copy, ported here
  verbatim as static/editable section settings. They are NOT read from
  and NOT wired to `print_calculator.pricing_config` (the shop metafield
  that actually drives checkout discounts) — this task has no access to
  that metafield's current live values and was not asked to reconcile
  against it. Whoever publishes this section should verify these
  displayed numbers still match whatever discount tiers are actually
  configured live, or customers will see marketing copy that promises a
  different discount than checkout applies. **

  Schema/rendering decisions:

  1. The 4 rungs are a repeatable `rung` block (percent number, spend_text
     text, bar_width range 0-100 matching the source's per-rung
     `--w:25%/50%/75%/100%` custom property) rather than 4 fixed settings,
     so a merchant can add/remove/reorder tiers — same block-loop pattern
     Task 8 (home-craft.liquid, spec_item blocks) and Task 12
     (home-shop-grid.liquid) used. The preset below ships the source's
     exact 4 rungs (5%/£10, 10%/£20, 15%/£30, 20%/£40) as the default.
     bar_width is exposed as its own editable field (not silently derived
     from percent) since the source's bar-fill values (25/50/75/100) don't
     follow an obvious formula from the percent values (5/10/15/20) — they
     look like "position in the ladder" (1st of 4 = 25%, ..., 4th of 4 =
     100%) rather than a function of the percent itself, so a merchant
     changing percent shouldn't silently change the fill width too.

  2. `eyebrow` is included as a section setting even though the outer task
     list names only heading/shipping_text/terms_text — every other ported
     homepage section with a `.sec__head` (home-craft, home-shop-grid,
     home-services) exposes its eyebrow as an editable setting for
     consistency, and the source's eyebrow here ("Spend more, save more")
     is clearly the same kind of merchant-editable microcopy as those.
     Flagged here as one field added beyond the literal list, same
     disclosure pattern Task 8 used for its `flip_label` addition.

  3. The heading keeps the source's `<br />` line break (`Discounts that
     stack<br />as your army grows.`) — unlike Task 8's craft heading,
     which dropped its manual break as arbitrary line-length balance, this
     break sits at a real clause boundary ("...as your army grows" is a
     complete trailing clause, not a mid-phrase wrap) so it's modelled as
     a `richtext` setting (matching home-shop-grid.liquid's `lede`
     precedent for the same reason: needing an editable line break)
     instead of dropping it. Flagged as a judgment call since the brief
     said "heading (text)".

  4. `.reveal`/`.reveal.in`/`data-d` scroll-reveal classes from the source
     are dropped from the markup, same call every other ported section in
     this project already made (see home-craft.liquid, home-shop-grid.liquid)
     — no reveal-on-scroll JS exists anywhere in this theme yet, so the
     bars would render permanently at `width:0` (the `.rung__bar i` default)
     and never reach `var(--w)` without `.reveal.in` ever being added. The
     CSS below renders the bar fill unconditionally instead of gating it
     behind a `.reveal.in` class that would never appear.
{% endcomment %}
<section class="sec sec--light" id="SpendSave-{{ section.id }}">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <p class="eyebrow on-light">{{ section.settings.eyebrow }}</p>
      <h2 class="display">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder">
      {% for block in section.blocks %}
        {% if block.type == 'rung' %}
          <div class="rung" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
            <div class="rung__pct">{{ block.settings.percent }}%</div>
            <div class="rung__spend">{{ block.settings.spend_text }}</div>
            <div class="rung__bar"><i style="--w:{{ block.settings.bar_width }}%"></i></div>
          </div>
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    <div class="shipflag">
      <svg width="21" height="21" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"><path d="M16 3h1.4a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 1l2.6 4.3a2 2 0 0 1 .3 1V17a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-1"/><path d="M2 6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h11a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v12H9"/><circle cx="7" cy="18" r="2"/><circle cx="17" cy="18" r="2"/></svg>
      {{ section.settings.shipping_text }}
    </div>
    <p class="terms">{{ section.settings.terms_text }}</p>
  </div>
</section>

